section.para:
The detector works on any character sequence. In the text below, cafÃ© is what café becomes when its UTF-8 bytes are read through the Windows-1252 decoding, and � is a replacement character left by a failed decode:
ArtifactDetector detector = new CursorArtifactDetector(); String text = "cafÃ© costs �8";
for (TextArtifact artifact : detector.detect(text)) { System.out.println(artifact.type() + " " + artifact.span()); }
This prints one line per finding, with exact offsets into the original text:
mojibake [3..5) replacement [12..13)
The reverse mapping covers both Windows-1252 and ISO-8859-1. The latter preserves bytes 0x80 through 0x9F as C1 controls, so damaged text such as the UTF-8 euro sign read through Latin-1 is still reported as one mojibake span rather than an unrelated control. Ordinary accented words are not mojibake: their single-byte images are not valid UTF-8 sequences, so a text like déjà vu yields no finding at all. ArtifactsManualExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.

The default constructor reports every built-in type. A caller can limit findings to selected types with the set constructor:
ArtifactDetector textDamage = new CursorArtifactDetector(Set.of( TextArtifact.TYPE_MOJIBAKE, TextArtifact.TYPE_UNICODE_TAG));
List<TextArtifact> damage = textDamage.detect(text);
The set must be non-empty and contain only the constants declared by TextArtifact. It is copied during construction, so later changes to the caller's set do not alter the detector. ArtifactsManualExampleTest mirrors the configuration shown here.

			ArtifactAnnotator adapts the detector to the document pipeline. It
			requires no other layer and provides opennlp:artifacts, one
			annotation per finding carrying its TextArtifact:

Document document = new ArtifactAnnotator()
    .annotate(Document.of("cafÃ© costs �8"));

List<Annotation<TextArtifact>> artifacts = document.get(ArtifactAnnotator.ARTIFACTS);
// two annotations; the first covers "Ã©" and carries type "mojibake"

			Because findings are exact spans on a layer, any layer consumer composes with
			them: masking replaces the covered characters, filtering drops annotations by
			type, and a cleanup step can rewrite the flagged stretches while recording its
			edits. Masking, for example, overwrites every covered character in place:

StringBuilder masked = new StringBuilder("cafÃ© costs �8");
for (Annotation<TextArtifact> artifact : artifacts) {
    for (int i = artifact.span().getStart(); i < artifact.span().getEnd(); i++) {
        masked.setCharAt(i, '#');
    }
}
// masked.toString(): "caf## costs #8"

			ArtifactsManualExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.
